course: myths
lesson: 15
title: Weather, lightning and storms
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Thunder cracks over the house and Grandad pats your hand: "Don't worry, lightning never strikes the same place twice!" It's a cosy thing to say in a storm, and it's wrong: tall buildings like the Empire State Building are struck again and again every year.
explain: Weather sayings are old and many sound wise: too cold to snow, red sky at night, a wet September means a hard winter. Some hold a bit of truth, some none, and you can test them with a diary and old records.
explain: Lightning myths are the ones that matter most, because a wrong idea can put you in danger: a tree feels like shelter from rain, but trees attract lightning; lying flat feels safe, but it isn't.
explain: The real safety rule is simple: when thunder roars, go indoors, or into a car with a metal roof, where the electricity flows round the metal shell to the ground; the rubber tyres have nothing to do with it.
explain: For the fun myths, look closely: raindrops aren't teardrop-shaped, "heat lightning" is just faraway lightning, and storms are named from a list, not after people who caused them.
fact: Lightning often strikes the same place, especially tall things; the Empire State Building is hit about 20 to 25 times a year.
fact: A car with a metal roof protects you from lightning because the electricity flows around the outside metal shell to the ground; the tyres are too thin to matter.
fact: Lying flat on the ground in a thunderstorm makes you more exposed to current through the ground; the safest places are indoors or in a metal-roofed car.
fact: Sheltering under a tree in a thunderstorm is dangerous, because tall trees attract lightning.
fact: It can snow at very low temperatures; very cold air holds little moisture, so heavy snow is less likely, but it still snows in Antarctica at minus 40.
fact: "Heat lightning" is ordinary lightning too far away for its thunder to reach you; sound travels about one kilometre every three seconds.
fact: "Red sky at night, shepherd's delight" is partly true in places like Britain, where weather usually comes from the west, but it isn't reliable everywhere.
fact: Small raindrops are round, and bigger ones are flattened at the bottom like a burger bun as they fall; none is shaped like a teardrop.
fact: Big, complicated snowflakes are almost certainly all different, but very simple tiny crystals can look identical, and a scientist made matching ones in a lab.
fact: Hurricanes get names from lists made in advance by the World Meteorological Organization, to make warnings clear; the Met Office names UK storms too.
fact: There is no reliable link between a wet September and a hard winter; even computer forecasts find seasons ahead hard to predict.
fact: A comforting saying is not a safety rule; for lightning, follow official advice such as the Met Office's.
exercise: Grandad says lightning never strikes the same place twice. What's true? | It often strikes the same place, especially tall things | It can strike any one place only once and then never again | It never strikes buildings | It strikes only open fields
exercise: About how many times a year is the Empire State Building struck? | About 20 to 25 times | Never | Once in a hundred years | Exactly once a year
exercise: Kieran's dad says the rubber tyres protect you in a car. What really protects you? | The metal shell carrying the electricity round you to the ground | The thick rubber tyres stopping the electricity reaching the ground | The car's windows | The car radio's aerial
exercise: Neve's friend says lie flat on the ground in a lightning storm. What's the best thing to do? | Get indoors or into a metal-roofed car | Lie flat in the middle of the field, low down | Stand under the tallest tree for some shelter | Hold up a metal umbrella to keep the rain off
exercise: Jamie wants to hide under a tree in a thunderstorm. Why is that dangerous? | Tall trees attract lightning | Trees fall over in every storm | Leaves catch fire from rain | Trees make the thunder louder
exercise: Granny says it's too cold to snow. What's true? | It can snow when very cold, just usually less | Snow only falls above freezing | Below minus ten no snow can ever fall from the sky | Cold air is full of extra snow
exercise: Where does snow fall even at minus 40? | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Amazon | Hawaii's beaches
exercise: Ollie's uncle says heat lightning is a special kind with no thunder. What is it really? | Ordinary lightning too far away to hear | Lightning made by hot summer air alone, no storm | Lightning that has no electricity inside it | Light from the setting Sun bouncing off clouds
exercise: How far does the sound of thunder travel in about three seconds? | About one kilometre | About one metre | About one hundred kilometres | It doesn't travel at all
exercise: Ava's grandad says "red sky at night, shepherd's delight". How true is it? | Partly true where weather comes from the west, like Britain | Perfectly true everywhere on Earth, whatever the wind is doing | Completely made up everywhere | True only for shepherds
exercise: Mark draws raindrops like teardrops. What shape are falling raindrops? | Round when small, bun-shaped when bigger | Pointy teardrops, just as in the drawings | Perfect little cubes, like tiny dice of ice | Long thin needles that stretch as they fall
exercise: Lola's Christmas card says no two snowflakes are ever the same. What's the full truth? | Big complex ones are almost surely different, but tiny simple ones can match | Every snowflake is identical | Snowflakes are all the same until they melt | Only snowflakes from the same cloud match
exercise: Finn's sister thinks hurricanes are named after people who caused them. Where do the names come from? | Lists made in advance to make warnings clear | The person who saw the storm first, as a prize | The people who caused the storm, as a punishment | A vote by the people the storm hit the hardest
exercise: Uncle Ian says a wet September means a hard winter. What's true? | There's no reliable link | It's a proven rule | A dry September means a hard winter | It works only in leap years
exercise: How could you test Uncle Ian's saying? | Compare old Septembers and winters in weather records | Ask him to promise it's true | Wait for one September and then see if that winter is hard | Look at a calendar
test: You're playing football in the park and hear thunder. Your mate says, "Shelter under that big oak!" What do you do? | Run to a building or a car, never under a tree | Shelter under the oak until it passes | Lie flat in the goalmouth | Keep playing, lightning never hits parks or football pitches
test: "Lightning already hit that church spire last year, so it's the safest spot now lol" What's wrong? | Tall things get struck again and again | Nothing, lightning never repeats a place | Churches can't be struck, they're protected | Only new buildings get struck, never old ones
test: Kieran's dad has the right idea but the wrong reason about cars. Why is it worth fixing? | A car without a metal roof, like a soft-top, wouldn't protect the same way | His reason is fine, rubber stops all lightning | Cars never get struck anyway | It doesn't matter at all
test: You see a flash and count 9 seconds before the thunder. About how far away is the lightning? | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 30 metres | Right overhead
test: On a hot evening you see flashes but no thunder. What would happen if you drove towards them? | You'd likely start to hear thunder as you got closer | The flashes would vanish forever | You'd find a special silent storm | You'd find a hot cloud with no electricity in it at all
test: Granny looks at the freezing clear sky: "Too cold to snow tonight." What's the most accurate reply? | Very cold air holds little water, so snow is less likely, but still possible | She's right, snow can't fall below zero | Cold makes snow certain | Snow only falls in summer
test: Ava's grandad swears by "red sky at night". How could you check it fairly? | Keep a sky diary for a month and compare the next day's weather | Look at one red sunset tonight and see whether tomorrow is sunny | Ask a shepherd | Google it once
test: A cartoon shows a storm called Hurricane Bob, and Bob gets arrested for causing it. What's the real story? | Names come from a list made in advance; nobody causes it | Bob is the scientist who made it | Storms are named after the angriest person nearby at the time | Bob won a competition to cause storms
test: Lola catches two tiny snow crystals that look exactly alike and says her card lied. What do you say? | Simple tiny crystals can match; big complex flakes almost never do | Her magnifier must be broken | Snowflakes are always identical anyway | One of them must be plastic
test: Grandad is calming you in a storm with his saying. What's the kind and safe response? | Stay indoors with him; it's a nice saying but not a safety rule | Go outside together to prove to him that his old saying is wrong | Tell him off for lying | Stand at the open window to watch
test: Your friend camping in a field says she'll lie flat if a storm comes. What do you tell her? | Lying flat is worse; get into a building or a metal-roofed car | Good idea, the lower the better | Climb the nearest tree so the lightning goes under you instead | Hold the tent pole to earth it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the incidence of lightning upon a given location? | Lightning frequently strikes the same location, tall structures especially | Lightning is incapable of striking a location more than once | Lightning strikes exclusively in open countryside | Lightning avoids structures previously struck
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the occupants of a metal-roofed motor vehicle are protected from lightning. | The current is conducted around the metal body to the ground | The rubber tyres insulate the vehicle entirely from the ground | The glass windows repel the electrical discharge | The engine absorbs the electrical energy
exam: It is given that a person in open country hears thunder. Select the response consistent with official safety advice. | The person is to seek shelter within a building or a metal-roofed vehicle | The person is to lie flat upon the ground | The person is to shelter beneath the tallest available tree until it passes | The person is to raise a metal object above the head
exam: Ascertain the nature of the phenomenon commonly termed heat lightning. | Ordinary lightning at a distance too great for its thunder to be heard | A distinct form of lightning produced by heat alone | Sunlight reflected from clouds after sunset | Lightning devoid of electrical charge
